            "body": "Class 10 ke [equation-of-a-line.md](../../../class10/maths/equation-of-a-line/equation-of-a-line.md) mein tumne seekha tha ki ek line ka slope m = tan θ = (y₂ - y₁)/(x₂ - x₁) hota hai, parallel lines ka slope barabar (m₁ = m₂) hota hai, aur perpendicular lines ke slopes ka product -1 (m₁ · m₂ = -1) hota hai.\n\nPar Class 11 mein hum ek bada sawaal poochhte hain: agar do lines na to parallel hon (angle 0°) aur na hi perpendicular (angle 90°), balki kisi bhi beech ke angle par kaatein — jaise 30°, 45°, ya 60° — to kya hum sirf unke slopes m₁ aur m₂ se unke beech ka exact angle nikaal sakte hain? Bilkul! Is topic mein hum slope ke Class 11 nuances (obtuse inclination, collinearity) aur **Angle Between Two Lines** ka master formula derive karenge!",
            "bodyHinglish": "Class 10 ke [equation-of-a-line.md](../../../class10/maths/equation-of-a-line/equation-of-a-line.md) mein tumne seekha tha ki ek line ka slope m = tan θ = (y₂ - y₁)/(x₂ - x₁) hota hai, parallel lines ka slope barabar (m₁ = m₂) hota hai, aur perpendicular lines ke slopes ka product -1 (m₁ · m₂ = -1) hota hai.\n\nPar Class 11 mein hum ek bada sawaal poochhte hain: agar do lines na to parallel hon (angle 0°) aur na hi perpendicular (angle 90°), balki kisi bhi beech ke angle par kaatein — jaise 30°, 45°, ya 60° — to kya hum sirf unke slopes m₁ aur m₂ se unke beech ka exact angle nikaal sakte hain? Bilkul! Is topic mein hum slope ke Class 11 nuances (obtuse inclination, collinearity) aur **Angle Between Two Lines** ka master formula derive karenge!"
